  "claims": [
    {
      "claim": "The most transferable L1 record, perindopril in fasted healthy and hypertensive subjects, reports absolute oral bioavailability of 66–95%, well above the 20% threshold.",
      "card_ids": ["C10"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "Perindoprilat after oral perindopril in cirrhotic patients retained 30±6% bioavailability, and normal volunteers were 19±7%, showing ACE-inhibitor prodrug/active exposure near or above threshold.",
      "card_ids": ["C08", "C26"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L3 oral exposure records for enalapril, enalaprilat, perindopril and perindoprilat show systemic Cmax and AUC after oral prodrug dosing, consistent with meaningful absorption.",
      "card_ids": ["C66", "C80", "C61", "C63"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L4 enalapril prodrug permeability in human iPS intestinal cells was 1.93×10^-6 cm/s with reported Fa 0.60, and the phosphinic-acid ACE inhibitor SQ-29852 had 60% human absorption, supporting the absorption component for the phosphinic-ester prodrug query.",
      "card_ids": ["C137", "C130"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L5 enalapril prodrug absorptive Papp in hiPSC-derived intestinal epithelial layers was 2.95±0.41×10^-6 cm/s, supporting intestinal absorption of ACE-inhibitor ester prodrugs.",
      "card_ids": ["C188"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L6 ramipril and quinapril, ACE-inhibitor ester prodrugs, showed low liver S9 intrinsic clearance (0.061 ml/min/mg S9 in human for ramipril; 0.17 in rat for quinapril), indicating low hepatic first-pass metabolic loss for this prodrug class.",
      "card_ids": ["C217", "C234"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L6 nateglinide, a carboxylic-acid/cyclohexyl oral drug, had low HLM intrinsic clearance (2.02 µL/min/mg), further supporting low hepatic clearance for the query's acid/cyclohexyl scaffold.",
      "card_ids": ["C204"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "Low-permeability records for enalaprilat and lisinopril apply to active diacid ACE inhibitors, not the ester prodrug query, and do not provide affirmative F<20% evidence.",
      "card_ids": ["C184", "C166", "C196", "C125", "C146"]
    }
  ],
  "new_evidence_assessment": [
    {
      "family": "hepatic_clearance_metabolic_stability",
      "applicability": "moderate",
      "direction": "supportive",
      "decision_effect": "strengthened",
      "card_ids": ["C217", "C234", "C204"]
    }
  ],
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct fosinopril-specific oral bioavailability, permeability, solubility, efflux, or hepatic clearance record is supplied; the decision relies on class analogy to perindopril, enalapril, ramipril, and quinapril.",
    "L6 hepatic clearance records are for ramipril, quinapril, and nateglinide, not fosinopril; species and assay differences (human vs rat liver S9, HLM) limit direct transfer.",
    "The query has no specified external condition, so cirrhosis-specific records may not transfer directly to a general population.",
    "Physicochemical prior indicates high molecular weight, high TPSA, and full ionization at pH 7.4, but no direct fosinopril measurement resolves this uncertainty.",
    "Perindoprilat normal-volunteer bioavailability (19±7%) is near the threshold, but this applies to the active diacid, not the ester prodrug query."
  ],
  "decision_summary": "Predicted high (F≥20%) because the most transferable L1 evidence for ACE-inhibitor prodrugs (perindopril 66–95%; perindoprilat 30±6% in cirrhosis) is above threshold; L3/L4/L5 add oral exposure and intestinal absorption for enalapril/perindopril prodrugs; L6 adds low hepatic intrinsic clearance for ramipril and quinapril, supporting low first-pass loss; no transferable affirmative F<20% evidence is present."
}

